+   unsigned wnum = task->num;
+
+   /*
+    * Input CBUF surface stride. A row costs inw * DIV_ROUND_UP(ic, 16) feature
+    * atomics of 16 bytes, and this counts them in 64 byte units, so it is
+    * inw * atomics / 4. Writing it as inw * ic / 64 is the same thing only
+    * when ic is a multiple of 16, and it truncates.
+    *
+    * Board: at 32 channels the truncation costs half a unit on every ODD
+    * width, and every odd width measured fails while every even one passes.
+    * dw32w71 is 2 of 32 channels and dw32w72 beside it is 32 of 32, one row
+    * window each and well inside the CBUF budget, so nothing else separates
+    * them. 64 channels never truncates, inw * 4 / 4 being exact, and 64
+    * channel layers fail for a different reason.
+    *
+    * This form reproduces the old value exactly on every case that passes
+    * today: 112 wide at 16, 32 and 64 channels, 96 wide at 16, 32 and 64, 44
+    * wide at 128, and 7 wide at 1024.
+    *
+    * ...and that is still not the whole rule; see rkt_entries_per_slice. A row
+    * whose last 64 byte unit would hold exactly three atomics is not packed at
+    * all and costs a whole unit per column, which rkt_task.c has always known
+    * and this file did not.
+    *
+    * That is exactly the population that fails: a pointwise at 48 input
+    * channels is 28 of 64 channels correct, at 33 it is 16 of 64, and both are
+    * three atomics per row. 64 channels is four atomics and 16 or 32 is one or
+    * two, and all of those come out identical under either form, which is why
+    * this survived until a 48 channel layer was run.
